# Line endings are decided here, not by each clone's core.autocrlf. Without this file a
# contributor with autocrlf=false commits CRLF, and the next Windows checkout rewrites
# every line of it — a whole-file diff that says nothing.
#
# text=auto: store LF in the repository, check out whatever the platform uses. Everything
# already committed is LF, so this changes no blob; it only pins the behaviour.
* text=auto

# Better hunk headers — git ships a C# function-name parser and only uses it when asked.
*.cs      text diff=csharp

# Shell scripts are executed by their line endings. A CRLF sh script fails with an
# unreadable error about a command name with a carriage return in it.
*.sh      text eol=lf

# The counterpart: Windows Powershell is happiest with CRLF, and this is a Windows repo
# whose only script is the ffmpeg fetcher.
*.ps1     text eol=crlf
*.cmd     text eol=crlf
*.bat     text eol=crlf

# Visual Studio writes these with CRLF whatever anyone else does.
*.sln     text eol=crlf
*.slnx    text eol=crlf

# Never normalise, never diff as text. None of these are in the tree yet; they are here so
# the first screenshot added to the README cannot be corrupted on the way in.
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.ico     binary
*.pdf     binary
*.zip     binary
*.7z      binary
*.dll     binary
*.exe     binary
*.pdb     binary
*.mp4     binary
*.mkv     binary
*.mov     binary
*.webm    binary
